Tenda AC10 V5 (Siflower SF19A2890) on OpenWrt
==============================================

This tree adds support for the Tenda AC10 V5 to OpenWrt main, on Linux
6.18 (the siflower target's kernel), with Ethernet, the MT7530 switch,
the GMAC hardware NAT engine, CPU frequency scaling and both WiFi bands.


Branches
--------

Two branches are provided. They are identical except for the WiFi
commit ("sfwifi: add the SF19A2890 WiFi driver and firmware"), which
builds the driver for one of the two known builds of the LMAC firmware:

  review/sdkfw    LMAC firmware from Siflower's MIT-licensed 1806 SDK
                  (the memory-optimised build). The firmware is MIT
                  licensed and can be redistributed.

  review/stockfw  LMAC firmware from the Tenda AC10 V5's original
                  firmware. Faster on WiFi, most of all on receive
                  (see Performance), but the firmware carries no
                  licence.

The two firmware builds number their messages differently and differ
in the TX confirm layout (0x20 vs 0x1c bytes), the VI queue size (32 vs
64) and the interface/station limits (8/32 vs 3/20), so a driver built
for one cannot run the other. A mismatch is reported at probe as
"unsupported LMAC firmware build".


Commits
-------

  kernel: netfilter: fix two flowtable offload bugs
  kernel: add kmod-dsa-mt7530
  siflower: sf19a2890: support CPU frequency scaling
  siflower: MIPS: prefetch in user copies and csum_partial
  siflower: spi-nor: add S25FL064K lock support
  siflower: dsa: add an 802.1Q-based tagger for MT7530
  siflower: stmmac: add hardware NAT, speed up the data path
  sfwifi: add the SF19A2890 WiFi driver and firmware
  siflower: add support for Tenda AC10 V5
  siflower: add Readme.TendaAC10V5

Kernel changes are carried as OpenWrt patches (target/linux/generic/
pending-6.18 and target/linux/siflower/patches-6.18) and driver sources
(target/linux/siflower/files-6.18). The WiFi driver is an out-of-tree
kernel package (package/kernel/sfwifi) built against the backports
cfg80211. Each commit message describes its change in detail.


Hardware
--------

  SoC       Siflower SF19A2890 (MIPS interAptiv, 2 cores x 2 threads),
            run at up to 1.1 GHz
  RAM       64 MiB
  Flash     8 MiB SPI NOR (Winbond W25Q64)
  Ethernet  MT7530 switch, 1x WAN + 3x LAN 10/100/1000
  WiFi      built in: 2.4 GHz 2x2 802.11n, 5 GHz 2x2 802.11ac
  UART      115200 8N1


Build
-----

Host: a Linux machine with the OpenWrt build prerequisites, see
https://openwrt.org/docs/guide-developer/toolchain/install-buildsystem

  git clone -b review/sdkfw \
      https://github.com/nsolanki-pro/openwrt-tendaAC10V5.git
  cd openwrt-tendaAC10V5          # use -b review/stockfw for the other
                                  # firmware build
  ./scripts/feeds update -a
  ./scripts/feeds install -a

  cat > .config <<'CFG'
  CONFIG_TARGET_siflower=y
  CONFIG_TARGET_siflower_sf19a2890=y
  CONFIG_TARGET_siflower_sf19a2890_DEVICE_tenda_ac10-v5=y
  CFG
  make defconfig
  make -j$(nproc) download
  make -j$(nproc)

The same selection can be made in "make menuconfig" (Target System:
Siflower, Subtarget: SF19A2890, Target Profile: Tenda AC10 V5). The
board selection is all that is needed: the device profile pulls in
every package the board needs. For the web interface, also add
"CONFIG_PACKAGE_luci=y" to .config before "make defconfig".

The first build also builds the host tools and the toolchain; later
builds are incremental.


What gets built
---------------

In bin/targets/siflower/sf19a2890/:

  openwrt-siflower-sf19a2890-tenda_ac10-v5-squashfs-factory.bin
      Kernel and root filesystem, checked against the 7488 KiB
      firmware partition. Used for the first install from U-Boot.
  openwrt-siflower-sf19a2890-tenda_ac10-v5-squashfs-sysupgrade.bin
      The same image with sysupgrade metadata. Used for upgrades from
      a running OpenWrt, including switching between the sdkfw and
      stockfw builds.
  openwrt-siflower-sf19a2890-tenda_ac10-v5-initramfs-kernel.bin
      Kernel with the root filesystem in RAM. Not needed for
      installation.
  openwrt-siflower-sf19a2890-tenda_ac10-v5.manifest
      The packages in the image, with versions.
  packages/, sha256sums, profiles.json, *.buildinfo
      The target's packages, checksums and build information.

With LuCI selected, the factory and sysupgrade images are about
7.06 MB (6.7 MiB) of the 7488 KiB (7.3 MiB) firmware partition; the
rest becomes the writable overlay.

Inside the image:
- Kernel: Linux 6.18 (LZMA uImage with the devicetree appended). Built
  in: GMAC with the hardware NAT engine, clocks, pinctrl, reset, DW APB
  timer and cpufreq, SPI NOR, thermal.
- Packages from the device profile: kmod-dsa-mt7530 (switch),
  kmod-sfwifi (sf16a18_rf, startcore and sf16a18_fmac modules, with
  the backports kmod-cfg80211 as a dependency), sfwifi-firmware (LMAC
  firmware and RF configuration), zram-swap.
- Target defaults: wpad-basic-mbedtls, firewall4 with kmod-nft-offload,
  uboot-envtools, dropbear and the usual OpenWrt base system.
- Board files: network and LED setup, the WiFi driver loader
  (init.d/sfwifi) and watchdog, packet steering, sysctl tuning and
  flow offloading on by default.

Flash layout (8 MiB SPI NOR):

  Offset     Size      Partition    Contents
  0x000000   32 KiB    spl-loader   bootloader, kept
  0x008000   412 KiB   u-boot       bootloader, kept
  0x06f000   4 KiB     u-boot-env   U-Boot environment
  0x070000   64 KiB    factory      MAC address and RF calibration, kept
  0x080000   7488 KiB  firmware     OpenWrt: kernel, squashfs, overlay
  0x7d0000   64 KiB    CFM          Tenda configuration, kept
  0x7e0000   64 KiB    CFM_BACKUP   Tenda configuration, kept
  0x7f0000   64 KiB    CFG          Tenda configuration, kept


Install
-------

First install, from the U-Boot console over TFTP (serial 115200 8N1;
stop autoboot with Ctrl+C):

  tftpboot 0x81000000 openwrt-siflower-sf19a2890-tenda_ac10-v5-squashfs-factory.bin
  sf probe 0
  sf update 0x81000000 0x80000 ${filesize}
  reset

The stock web UI and the U-Boot web failsafe only accept signed Tenda
images, so they cannot be used for the first install.

Upgrades, from OpenWrt (keeps the configuration):

  sysupgrade -v /tmp/openwrt-siflower-sf19a2890-tenda_ac10-v5-squashfs-sysupgrade.bin


Performance
-----------

Three builds are compared, all on this board:

  Vendor    the Tenda original firmware (Linux 4.14)
  sdkfw     review/sdkfw on Linux 6.18 (SDK LMAC firmware)
  stockfw   review/stockfw on Linux 6.18 (Tenda LMAC firmware)

sdkfw and stockfw were measured back to back on 2026-10-14 with the
same script, setup and configuration; each value is the range of two
runs. The vendor firmware was measured earlier (wired 2026-10-03, WiFi
2026-10-08) with the same iperf3 binary (3.20), server and AP. All
values are in Mbit/s; "-" means not measured.

Setup:

                   upstream network (home gateway)
                                 |
                                 | 1 GbE (wan port, bridged)
     +---------------------------+---------------------------+
     | Server: TP-Link EX220 v2 running OpenWrt              |
     | - iperf3 3.20 server                                  |
     | - AP for the station tests: 5 GHz channel 36 VHT80,   |
     |   2.4 GHz channel 9 HT40                              |
     +---------------------------+----------------+----------+
                                 | 1 GbE (lan2)   )))
                                 |                )))  WiFi (test 3)
                                 | WAN, DHCP      )))
     +---------------------------+----------------+----------+
     | Device under test: Tenda AC10 V5                      |
     | - router mode: NAT, firewall, HW + SW flow offloading |
     | - its own APs: 5 GHz channel 149 VHT80, 2.4 GHz       |
     |   channel 1 HT20 (moved to 36 / 9 for test 3)         |
     | - station interfaces added only for test 3            |
     +---------------------------+---------------------------+
                                 | 1 GbE (lan3)
                                 | LAN, DHCP
     +---------------------------+---------------------------+
     | LAN client: Raspberry Pi 3B+ (USB 2.0 Gigabit         |
     | Ethernet), iperf3 client                              |
     +-------------------------------------------------------+

  Test 1  router  --wan-->  server             (iperf3 on the router)
  Test 2  client  --lan3--> router (NAT) --wan--> server
  Test 3  router station  ~~WiFi~~>  server AP  (iperf3 on the router)

- The LAN client's USB 2.0 Ethernet tops out at about 276 up / 319
  down on its own, so the NAT results are capped by the client, not by
  the router.
- Hardware and software flow offloading are on (the default) and the
  CPU runs at up to 1.1 GHz (schedutil). For test 2 the offload mode
  is switched with the firewall's flow_offloading options.
- The measured images also had the kernel profiler enabled (profile=4
  on the command line, about 1.5 MB of RAM), which is not part of these
  branches.

1. Router-terminated traffic over the WAN port
   (iperf3 on the router, 15 s runs; down = server to router)

     Test                 Vendor        sdkfw         stockfw
     TCP 1 flow, down     302           603-631       585-597
     TCP 1 flow, up       329           424-429       377-399
     TCP 4 flows, down    -             641-659       658-665
     TCP 4 flows, up      -             588-593       569-594
     UDP down             139           136-141       136-154
     UDP up               154           70-179        193-194

   UDP is offered at 600 Mbit/s with 1400-byte datagrams; the receiver
   rate is shown. Loss on OpenWrt: down 0.1-1.2%, up 8-66% (the sender
   outruns the path). The vendor values are averages of 5 (TCP) and 3
   (UDP) runs; the vendor firmware cannot run iperf3 with several
   flows.

2. NAT forwarding, LAN client to WAN server
   (4 TCP flows, 15 s runs; busy = busiest router CPU)

     Mode                  Build     Up        Down      Busiest CPU
     Hardware NAT (HNAT)   sdkfw     278       312-313   3-4%
                           stockfw   278       313       3-5%
     Software flowtable    sdkfw     276-277   294-304   51-62%
                           stockfw   278       306-311   55-65%
     No offload (CPU NAT)  sdkfw     179-187   221-249   76-78%
                           stockfw   173-175   220-255   77-79%

   All modes reach the LAN client's limit except CPU NAT. Hardware NAT
   does it with the CPUs nearly idle. The vendor firmware's NAT
   forwarding was not measured.

3. WiFi, router as a station to the server AP
   (one TCP flow, 10 s runs; TX = router to server)

     Test            Vendor     sdkfw       stockfw
     5 GHz TX        181        148-236     265
     5 GHz RX        63         125-131     294-345
     2.4 GHz TX      3.0-4.0    183-189     200-201
     2.4 GHz RX      2.7-3.5    137-142     184

   Link: 5 GHz VHT80 MCS7 2 streams (585 Mbit/s), 2.4 GHz HT40 MCS15
   (270 Mbit/s). The vendor firmware was measured as a station on the
   same AP (5 GHz on channel 36) with 15 s runs; on 2.4 GHz it
   transmitted at 1 Mbit/s legacy rates.

sdkfw compared with stockfw:
- Wired results (tests 1 and 2) are the same within run-to-run noise:
  they do not depend on the WiFi firmware build.
- WiFi: the Tenda LMAC firmware build is faster in every test, most of
  all on receive: 5 GHz RX 294-345 vs 125-131 (about 2.5x), 2.4 GHz RX
  184 vs 137-142 (about 1.3x). It also needed far fewer TCP
  retransmissions: 5 in all eight WiFi runs, against 107.

Both OpenWrt builds are well ahead of the vendor firmware: about 2x on
wired TCP download and 1.1-1.3x on upload, and far ahead as a WiFi
station.


Known limitations
-----------------

- The HNAT engine offloads IPv4 TCP/UDP with SNAT/masquerade and a
  single WAN. Traffic to and from WiFi clients uses the software
  flowtable.
- The HNAT engine has no per-flow hit counters, so offloaded flows use a
  300 s flowtable TCP timeout instead of hardware statistics.
- The WiFi driver chain is loaded once per boot (init.d/sfwifi).
  Unloading and reloading it wedges the LMAC firmware.
- The core voltage is fixed. cpufreq only changes the CPU clock
  divider.
